.\" Man page for Vuurmuur 0.8.2. Kept in sync with the option handling in
.\" vuurmuur/vuurmuur.c (optstring, prog_opts and print_help()).
.TH "VUURMUUR" "8" "February 2025" "Vuurmuur 0.8.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vuurmuur \- rule creating daemon of the Vuurmuur IPTables frontend.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vuurmuur \fB[\-bCdDfFhKklnVtv]\fR \fB[\-c\fR \fIconfig\-file\fR \fB][\-d\fR \fIdebug\-level\fR \fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBvuurmuur\fR
is the daemon that turns the objects maintained by
.BR vuurmuur_script (8)
and
.BR vuurmuur_conf (8)
into an iptables ruleset and loads it into the kernel. It also keeps the
ruleset loaded: started with
.B \-D
it stays resident and reloads when the configuration tool tells it the
backend changed.
.PP
Without
.B \-D
(or
.BR \-l )
vuurmuur loads the ruleset once and then exits. This is the mode
.B \-b
and the init scripts use for a single (re)load.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-b\fR, \fB\-\-bash\fR
Write a shell script to stdout instead of loading anything. As a side effect
this disables the iptables capability check, like \fB\-t\fR does.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR \fIlevel\fR, \fB\-\-debug\fR \fIlevel\fR
Enable debugging, 1 low, 3 high. Output goes to the debug log file, see
.B FILES
below. Levels above the compiled in maximum abort with an error.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-configfile\fR \fIfile\fR
Use \fIfile\fR instead of the default configuration file. With
.B \-v
the name of the file in use is printed.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Give a summary of the command line options and exit.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version of vuurmuur and libvuurmuur in use and exit.
.TP
\fB\-l\fR
Deprecated spelling of \fB\-D\fR.
.TP
\fB\-D\fR, \fB\-\-daemon\fR
Load the ruleset and stay running in the background. vuurmuur detaches with
\fBdaemon\fR(3) called with \fInochdir\fR and \fInoclose\fR set, so its
standard input, output and error streams stay attached to the terminal it was
started from.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-foreground\fR
Only meaningful with \fB\-D\fR: run the reload loop without daemonizing. Use
this to keep the process in the foreground of a supervisor.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Verbose output.
.TP
\fB\-K\fR, \fB\-\-killme\fR
Ask the running daemon to stop by sending \fBSIGTERM\fR to the pid read from
the pid file. Exits 0 if a daemon was found and signalled, non zero
otherwise. This option needs no other arguments and takes effect immediately.
.TP
\fB\-k\fR, \fB\-\-keep\fR
Do not unlink the temporary ruleset file that iptables-restore loads, so it
can be inspected after the run. Files land in \fI/tmp\fR, see
.B FILES
below. Useful for debugging a ruleset the kernel refused.
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-no-check\fR
Do not probe which iptables capabilities this kernel offers and assume all of
them are supported.
.TP
\fB\-C\fR, \fB\-\-clear-vuurmuur\fR
Remove the rules vuurmuur installed and set the INPUT, OUTPUT and FORWARD
policies to ACCEPT. Chains whose name starts with \fBPRE\-VRMR\-\fR are left
in place. Use with care: the machine is left without a firewall.
.TP
\fB\-F\fR, \fB\-\-clear-all\fR
Flush \fIall\fR iptables rules and chains, including \fBPRE\-VRMR\-\fR and any
chain installed by other software, and set the three policies to ACCEPT. Use
with care.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-force-start\fR
Start even when no rules are defined. Without this option vuurmuur refuses to
start in that case, because loading an empty ruleset would lock the machine
out.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on success, non zero on failure. \fB\-K\fR exits non zero when no running
daemon was found.
.SH "SIGNALS"
.TP
\fBSIGHUP\fR
Reload the configuration and the objects from the backend and reinstall the
ruleset. This is the hook the log rotation script uses.
.TP
\fBSIGINT\fR, \fBSIGTERM\fR
Shut down cleanly, removing the shared memory segment and the pid file.
.PP
Note that \fBSIGHUP\fR is handled by vuurmuur only. It does not reach
.BR vuurmuur_log (8),
whose zone and service name tables then stay stale, except when vuurmuur
notices that a dynamic interface changed address. Use
.BR vuurmuur_script (8)
\fB\-\-reload\fR, or signal both daemons, to reload the whole setup.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/vuurmuur/config.conf
Configuration file, unless another one was given with \fB\-c\fR. The
\fIetcdir\fR part is fixed at build time unless \fBVUURMUUR_SYSCONFDIR\fR is
set; see ENVIRONMENT.
.TP
.I /var/run/vuurmuur.pid
Pid file. It holds the process id and the id of the shared memory segment.
.TP
.I /tmp/vuurmuur-XXXXXX
The generated ruleset, in iptables-restore format. Removed at exit unless
\fB\-k\fR was given.
.TP
.I /tmp/vuurmuur-load-result-XXXXXX
What iptables-restore wrote to its error stream while loading the ruleset.
.TP
.I /tmp/vuurmuur-shape-XXXXXX
The generated traffic shaping script, when shaping is configured.
.TP
.I <logdir>/vuurmuur.log\fR, \fI<logdir>/error.log\fR, \fI<logdir>/debug.log
The three logs vuurmuur writes itself, where \fI<logdir>\fR is the
.B LOGDIR
setting from the configuration file, \fI/var/log/vuurmuur\fR unless that setting
says otherwise.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VUURMUUR_SYSCONFDIR
Overrides the compiled-in configuration directory: the config file is then
.IR $VUURMUUR_SYSCONFDIR /vuurmuur/config.conf
and the textdir backend's object tree moves with it, because the backend finds
it through the configuration. A warning is logged whenever the variable is in
use. The same variable applies to
.BR vuurmuur_conf (8),
.BR vuurmuur_log (8)
and
.BR vuurmuur_script (8),
so a run with it set can load rules from a tree the running daemons never see.
.SH HISTORY
Vuurmuur writes no syslog output: it keeps its own log files, and
.BR vuurmuur_log (8)
reads packets from the netfilter log target rather than from a system log. The
\fB\-L\fR and \fB\-X\fR letters from the 0.5.x releases are still in the option
string with no handler and no \fBdefault:\fR arm, so they are accepted silently
and do nothing. The long form \fB\-\-loglevel\fR is gone from the option table,
so it does reach the error path: \fBvuurmuur: unrecognized option\fR appears on
stderr and the run then continues with whatever else was given.
